WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.694
White noise has the same power in every hertz: its spectral density is flat, just as white light contains every color.

2
00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:18.798
But each octave has twice as many hertz as the one below. That is why the power per octave of white noise rises 3 dB per octave and it sounds high and hissy.

3
00:00:19.495 --> 00:00:28.615
Pink noise has the same power in every octave: its density falls 3 dB per octave. It sounds balanced, and it is used to measure rooms and loudspeakers.

4
00:00:31.163 --> 00:00:40.788
Brown noise falls 6 dB per octave in density, that is, 3 dB per octave in power. It concentrates its energy in the lows and sounds like distant thunder.
